anary does not prevent the crash; this is a .bss OOB access, not stack overflow.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1705\",\"description\":\"PoC: edit_cookie with idx=-7 caused SIGSEGV (signal 11). Return code = -11. Crash at 0x1705 (movzx eax, BYTE PTR [rax]) where rax = NULL + read_return(-1) = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-7, read(0, NULL, size) was called. rsi=0x0 (NULL buffer), rdx=size. read() returned -1 (EFAULT). Then buf[-1] dereference caused SIGSEGV.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (NULL + read_return(-1))\",\"return_code\":-11,\"details\":\"PoC verified CRASH: edit_cookie with idx=-7 caused SIGSEGV (signal 11, return code -11). msg[-7] = NULL (0x4028), read(0, NULL, size) returned -1, then buf[-1] = *(NULL + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) caused SIGSEGV at 0x1705.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"CRASH is VERIFIED. The crash is a direct consequence of the OOB signedness bug: idx=-7 selects a NULL pointer from the gap between stdout and stdin FILE* pointers, causing a NULL pointer dereference.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-004\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch allows OOB indexing. edit_cookie with negative idx calls read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1), which semantically writes attacker data to the OOB pointer target. However, read() fails with EFAULT in all tested configurations because the size is either 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (when msg_size[idx]=0) or ~1.4GB (when msg_size[idx] aliases a heap pointer's low 4 bytes).\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with idx=-8 loads msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (valid writable address) and calls read(0, stdout_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1). The write target is the stdout FILE structure, but read() returns -1 (EFAULT) because the size exceeds user space limits.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf(%llu) + jl signed comparison\",\"Negative idx bypasses signed bounds check\",\"msg[idx] loads OOB pointer (stdout FILE* at idx=-8)\",\"msg_size[idx] loads OOB size (0 at idx=-8, or heap pointer low bytes after creating cookies)\",\"read(0, stdout_ptr, size-1) called but returns -1 (EFAULT)\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: semantically writes to OOB pointer target, but read() EFAULT prevents actual write\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB write target (stdout FILE* at idx=-8) and write data (attacker-controlled stdin input)\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides idx=-8 via scanf(%llu) and write data via read(0, ...). The write target is msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (fixed relative offset), not arbitrary address.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., -8 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the signed jl comparison\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be > 0 (initial value is 5)\",\"necessity\":\"The jl check compares idx against cookie_num; negative idx < positive cookie_num passes\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must contain a valid writable pointer (stdout FILE* at idx=-8 is a valid libc address)\",\"necessity\":\"read(0, ptr, size) needs a valid writable buffer address; stdout FILE* is writable\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx]-1 must be a reasonable size that passes the kernel's access_ok check\",\"necessity\":\"read() with size 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF or ~1.4GB fails with EFAULT because buffer+size exceeds user space limit\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"Alternative: create 26+ cookies so msg_size[-1] aliases msg[31] high 4 bytes = 0x00005555 (21845, small positive), but msg[-1] = NULL (no valid write target)\",\"necessity\":\"The alias trick provides a small positive size, but the corresponding pointer is NULL, preventing the write\",\"verified\":false}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-8] = *(0x4160 + (-8)*4) = *(0x4140) = msg[28] low 4 bytes\",\"alias\":\"Low 4 bytes of msg[28] heap pointer (e.g., 0x555598b0 = ~1.4GB)\",\"implication\":\"After creating 26 cookies, msg_size[-8] = 0x555598b0, making read() size = 0x555598af (~1.4GB) → EFAULT\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-1] = *(0x4160 + (-1)*4) = *(0x415c) = msg[31] high 4 bytes\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[31] heap pointer (0x00005555 = 21845, small positive)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-1] = 21845 provides a small positive size, but msg[-1] = NULL (0x4058, gap before msg[0]), so read(0, NULL, 21844) → EFAULT\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-7] = *(0x4144) = msg[28] high 4 bytes = 0x00005555\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[28] heap pointer (21845, small positive)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-7] = 21845 provides small positive size, but msg[-7] = NULL (0x4028, gap between stdout and stdin), so read(0, NULL, 21844) → EFAULT\"},{\"expression\":\"Pointer/size dimension cross-analysis: even negative indices (-8,-6,-4) have valid pointers (FILE*) but large sizes; odd negative indices (-7,-5,-3,-1) have small sizes but NULL pointers\",\"alias\":\"No single negative index has both valid pointer AND reasonable size simultaneously\",\"implication\":\"The RELATIVE_WRITE primitive is semantically present (attacker controls write target via OOB index and write data via stdin) but cannot be dynamically verified because read() fails with EFAULT in all configurations\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Attempt edit_cookie with various negative indices to verify if read() can successfully write to OOB targets. Test with and without creating additional cookies to modify msg_size aliasing.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86-64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"read() return value (expected -1 = EFAULT for all configurations)\",\"GDB verification of read() arguments: rsi (buffer), rdx (size)\",\"Memory dump before/after read() to confirm no write occurred\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base, but OOB offsets are relative to msg[] in .bss. The write target (stdout FILE*) is a libc address, not affected by PIE.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but this write targets FILE structures in libc's data segment, not GOT. RELRO does not directly prevent this write (the write itself is prevented by EFAULT).\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution, not data writes. No direct impact on RELATIVE_WRITE.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack, not .bss or libc data. No impact on this primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie loads msg_size[idx] (lea rdx, [rax*4+0x0]; mov eax, [rdx+msg_size]), subtracts 1 (sub eax, 0x1), sign-extends (movsxd rdx, eax), loads msg[idx] (lea rcx, [rax*8+0x0]; mov rax, [rcx+msg]), and calls read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1). Semantically a write to msg[idx] target.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-8, read(0, 0x7ffff7fa75c0, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) called. rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*), rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size from msg_size[-8]=0, sub 1, movsxd). read() returned -1 (EFAULT). errno=14 (EFAULT). No write occurred.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: after creating 26 cookies, msg_size[-8] = 0x555598b0 (low 4 bytes of msg[28] heap pointer). read(0, stdout_ptr, 0x555598af) returned -1 (EFAULT). Buffer+size = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 + 0x555598af exceeds user space limit.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-7 (after 26 creates), msg_size[-7] = 0x00005555 (21845, small positive from msg[28] high 4 bytes). msg[-7] = NULL (0x4028). read(0, NULL, 21844) returned -1 (EFAULT). NULL buffer causes EFAULT.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"\",\"description\":\"Cross-analysis of all negative indices: even indices (-8,-6,-4) have valid FILE* pointers but large sizes (0 or 0x5555XXXX → EFAULT); odd indices (-7,-5,-3,-1) have small sizes (0x5555=21845) but NULL pointers (→ EFAULT). No single negative index has both valid pointer AND reasonable size, preventing the write from succeeding.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-004 is THEORETICAL. The OOB write semantics exist (edit_cookie calls read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) with OOB idx), but read() fails with EFAULT in all tested configurations. When msg_size[idx]=0 (initial state), size=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → EFAULT. After creating 26 cookies, msg_size[-8]=0x555598b0 (~1.4GB) → EFAULT. When size is small (0x5555=21845 at odd indices), pointer is NULL → EFAULT. The write cannot be dynamically verified.\"},\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"notes\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE is THEORETICAL. The signedness bug creates the semantic write primitive (attacker controls write target via OOB index and write data via stdin), but the kernel's access_ok check prevents read() from writing because: (1) when msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (too large); (2) when msg_size[idx] aliases heap pointer low bytes, size is ~1.4GB (too large); (3) when size is small (0x5555 from heap pointer high bytes), the corresponding msg[idx] pointer is NULL. The two-step write upgrade (modifying msg[idx] to point to an arbitrary address) is not possible because msg[idx] entries at negative indices are either FILE* pointers (not modifiable via normal operations) or NULL gaps (not writable).\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_READ\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"The OOB read via read_cookie is RELATIVE_READ, not ARB_READ. The read target is determined by the negative index (fixed offset from msg[] base), accessing specific global variables (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE*). The attacker cannot specify an arbitrary address Y to read from; they can only read at fixed negative offsets from msg[].\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"The OOB write via edit_cookie is RELATIVE_WRITE (THEORETICAL), not ARB_WRITE. The write target is determined by the negative index (fixed offset from msg[] base), not by an attacker-controlled address. Additionally, the write fails with EFAULT in all configurations, so even RELATIVE_WRITE cannot be dynamically verified. The two-step write upgrade is not possible because msg[idx] entries at negative indices are either FILE* pointers (not modifiable) or NULL gaps (not writable).\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether there exists a negative index configuration where both msg[idx] (pointer) is non-NULL and msg_size[idx] (size) is a small positive value that passes access_ok\",\"reason\":\"All tested configurations show a dimension mismatch: valid pointers have large/zero sizes, small sizes have NULL pointers. A different cookie creation count or heap layout might produce a favorable aliasing, but this requires further investigation.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Systematic enumeration of msg_size values for all negative indices across different cookie creation counts (0-27), checking if any configuration produces both a non-NULL msg[idx] and a small positive msg_size[idx]\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the INFO_LEAK can be extended to leak full libc addresses (not just the 4-byte _flags field)\",\"reason\":\"printf(%s) stops at the first null byte. The FILE structure _flags field (0xfbad2887) has null bytes at offset 4, limiting the leak to 4 bytes. The _IO_read_ptr and other pointer fields contain libc addresses but are beyond the null byte.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Investigate if a different OOB target (not a FILE structure) has contiguous non-null bytes that would leak full addresses, or if the FILE structure can be manipulated to remove null bytes before the pointer fields\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
